X: 1
T: La Cumparsita
C: Gerardo Matos Rodríguez, 1916
O: Uruguay
R: Tango
N: "The little carnival parade". Written by a Montevideo student for a
N: carnival troupe and first played at the Café La Giralda in 1917; it became
N: the best-known tango in the world.
N: Melody from the flute setting on flutetunes.com, checked note by note
N: against the mu-tech.org arrangement (WorldTrad/La_Cumparsita), which also
N: gives the chords. Where flutetunes writes Bb-A as grace notes (A bar 15,
N: B bars 1 and 9) mu-tech plays them as sixteenths, and that is used here.
N: B bar 2 follows flutetunes (Bb); mu-tech has A there.
N: Note values doubled from the 2/4 source so the moving value is an eighth.
N: Transposed down a fourth from G minor to D minor. The A strain then fits
N: the recorder exactly (C#4 to A5); B and C climb to E6 in the source, so
N: notes above A5 are taken down an octave (the octave leaps in B bars 3, 5
N: and 6 become repeated notes, C bars 1 and 5 move down whole) and the
N: low A3 pickups go up an octave.
N: Played A-B-C; the source form is A-B-A-C-A.
N: The accompaniment uses a tango 3-3-2 pattern (%%MIDI gchord).
L: 1/8
Q: 1/4=120
%%MIDI gchord bzzczzcz
M: 4/4
K: Dm
